Dear Flower team,

I spent ten years owning the commercial side of renewable assets in Germany: heading asset management of a solar
portfolio above USD 100M, building the yield and valuation models, and running the O&M due diligence on behalf of project
managers. The two things your posting puts at the centre — availability and cost — are the two things that portfolio was
judged on. Downtime was never an abstraction; it was a line in a budget someone had to explain.

The part of the job I did most often is the part your description spells out: *holding service providers to what they
signed*. Scoping an O&M contract, testing performance against its guarantees, and being the counterparty who notices when
a penalty clause should actually be applied. I have also sat on the other side, carrying the P&L, which is why I read a
service contract as a risk document rather than an administrative one.

I will be straight about what is new, because a role that owns availability for a battery fleet is not a place to
discover a surprise in month three. My assets were solar PV, not battery storage. The contract structures, the
availability arithmetic and the cost-per-MWh reasoning transfer; the hardware, the degradation behaviour and the revenue
stack of a BESS — ancillary services, capacity markets — do not transfer for free, and I would expect to spend my first
months inside the technology. I have not owned a permitting file myself either, though I have worked for a decade with the
people who do, and I know where the regulatory risk sits in an EEG and BImSchG context. Bazefield I have not used; the
monitoring I built was my own, on fifteen-minute telemetry with alerts on bad state and on silence.

What transfers without translation is the counterparty work. Grid operators, municipalities, authorities, landowners and
banks were my recurring calls, in German, and the follow-up after the call was mine as well. German is C1 and English
native, which is the combination this role needs: authorities and service providers on one side, a Stockholm team on the
other.

Your argument — flexible power, assets that earn by being reliable — is the commercial case I have been making about
renewable assets for a decade, from the other side of the trade. A storage fleet is the version of that argument where
availability is not a KPI on a slide but the revenue line itself, and that is exactly why the role interests me.

Two questions I would want to ask early: how availability is defined per asset today, contractual or measured, and where
the O&M penalty clauses have actually bitten. The answer to the second usually says more about a portfolio than the
first.

Kind regards,
#v(0.5cm)
Matt Ji
